1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,599
Inside one harbor booking request, you
load Booking id 500 twice — once to
authorize the trucker, once to attach a
gate pass. Do you get two Java objects or
one? In JPA, inside one persistence
context, you get one.

2
00:00:15,719 --> 00:00:25,607
That identity guarantee is the persistence
context’s quiet contract. The persistence
context is the first-level cache and the
unit-of-work boundary.

3
00:00:25,727 --> 00:00:32,903
It tracks managed entities, their
snapshots for dirty checking, and pending
inserts, updates, and deletes until flush.

4
00:00:33,024 --> 00:00:39,960
In a typical Spring Boot app it is
transaction-scoped: open when the
transactional method starts, close when it
ends.

5
00:00:40,079 --> 00:00:53,640
Open-session-in-view can stretch it across
the whole web request — convenient for
lazy reads, dangerous when it hides N+1
until production. Look at this java for a
moment. Look at this java for a moment.

6
00:00:53,759 --> 00:01:06,816
first == second is reference equality, not
merely equal ids. Hibernate did not need a
second SELECT for the findById after the
entity was already managed — the context
returned the tracked instance.

7
00:01:06,935 --> 00:01:15,743
Change fields through either variable;
there is only one snapshot to dirty-check.
Across transactions the guarantee
disappears.

8
00:01:15,863 --> 00:01:22,271
A booking loaded in request A and a
booking loaded in request B are different
instances even for the same row.

9
00:01:22,391 --> 00:01:30,072
That is why detached-edit bugs from the
previous lesson appear: people pass
instances across contexts as if identity
were global.

10
00:01:30,191 --> 00:01:43,391
EntityManager.clear() detaches all managed
entities — useful in batch jobs,
catastrophic mid-request if you still hold
references you plan to dirty-check.
flush() pushes SQL early without closing
the context.

11
00:01:43,511 --> 00:01:47,471
detach(entity) removes one instance from
tracking.

12
00:01:47,591 --> 00:01:54,407
The persistence context is not the second-
level cache, not the database session in
the JDBC sense alone,

13
00:01:54,527 --> 00:02:03,071
and not "wherever Hibernate feels like
remembering things." It is a well-defined
map of managed entities tied to a unit of
work.

14
00:02:03,191 --> 00:02:15,719
Believing two finds always mean two
SELECTs misses the cache. Believing two
finds across transactions share an
instance misses the boundary. You
understand the room managed entities live
in.

15
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:28,391
You still do not want every use case to
inject EntityManager and write find/save
by hand. Spring Data JPA repositories are
the typed façade — and for vessels, that
starts with findByImoNumber.
